A1. An island in the Pacific Ocean inhabited by American gobs and tourists. ~ JAPAN
A5. Wall street thrives on these. ~ LAMBS
A9. A flapper's delight. ~ SHEIK
A13. This makes peaches green. ~ ENVY
A15. A baby's delight. ~ TOE
A16. Chief reason for an American business man's trip to Germany. ~ BEER
A17. Policeman's method of escorting inebriate bums to the cooler. ~ DRAG
A19. One who gets even eventually. ~ AVENGER
A22. A rivulet. ~ RILL
A24. What you would like to do to a $100 bill. ~ GET
A25. Something to do if at first you don't succeed. ~ TRY
A26. A henpecked husband's refuge. ~ DEN
A28. As Cæsar said, "This is nothing." ~ NIL
A29. Prefix meaning into. ~ EN
A30. When the wages of this are paid some people will get time and a half for overtime. ~ SIN
A31. A condition of sidewalks in winter. ~ ICY
A33. This makes the bald ones look younger. ~ WIG
A35. State once famous for fast horses and mint juleps (abr.). ~ KY
A36. The stage-door Johnny stare. ~ OGLE
A38. What a hobo does. ~ IDLES
A39. First name of a famous movie actress. ~ BEBE
A42. Annoying Youth (init.). ~ AY
A44. The way Mr. Volstead voted, on the Volstead Act. ~ YEA
A46. A famous maker of pies. ~ MA
A48. This is the girl of the green gables. ~ ANN
A50. Famous country trisected by Cæsar. ~ GAUL
A52. This is harmless when the proprietor is tipped off. ~ RAID
A55. How Louis XVI became acquainted with his lady friends. ~ MET
A57. Why his sweetie went away. ~ NEGLECT
A58. Some people think this man has a lead pipe cinch. (We're inclined to agree!) ~ PLUMBER
A59. The kind of a hat all women want. ~ NEW
A60. What mere man tries to make both ends do. (Try and do it!) ~ MEET
A62. A joint made by hammering and heating. (Apartment houses have lots of hammering, but not so much heating.) ~ WELD
A63. A girl's name. ~ INA
A64. Patron saint of the wets. ~ AL
A66. If you dance you must either pay the fiddle or do this. ~ OWE
A68. Indefinite article. ~ AN
A69. How most blonds got that way. ~ DYED
A71. Purveyor of patriotic patter. ~ COHAN
A72. Best People on Earth (ask any Elk) (abbr.). ~ BPOE
A74. A part of the Western Hemisphere (abbr.). ~ CA
A75. How you feel on pay day. ~ GAY
A77. Foundation of flivvers. ~ TIN
A78. What prize fighters playfully place on an opponent's proboscis. ~ MIT
A79. This is always for rent (abbr.). ~ RM
A81. Minister's total of one and one. ~ ONE
A83. A hick. ~ YAP
A85. "It's mightier than the sword," sighed the convict when he broke his knife blade trying to dig his way out. ~ PEN
A86. Home of Spanish fish. ~ RIO
A87. Artificially produced. ~ MADE
A89. Those who kick. ~ KICKERS
A92. A famous cartoonist and a five-cent cigar. ~ WEED
A93. When in New York you can buy tickets for "Desire Under These." ~ ELMS
A95. Over (poetical). ~ OER
A96. In the near future. ~ SOON
A98. A sarcastic person. (Dumb Dora thinks this is a place to wash dishes!) ~ CYNIC
A99. Kingly. ~ ROYAL
A100. Something the Swiss do and get away with that other people get shot for even attempting. ~ YODEL

D1. The man you say good morning to. ~ JUDGE
D2. Decayed vegetable matter used as fuel. ~ PEAT
D3. What a cockney thinks a murderer should do. ~ ANG
D4. New version (abbr.). ~ NV
D6. What the members of the round table did when the twelve o'clock whistle blew. ~ ATE
D7. An Englishman looks well with one. ~ MONOCLE
D8. Something beggars do that whales don't. ~ BEG
D9. Direction a souse will be headed one minute after he has started walking northwest (ablr.). ~ SE
D10. Whose Izzy is he? ~ HER
D11. The land of shillalah's and brickbats. ~ ERIN
D12. The man who tires, also the old dun derby. ~ KELLY
D14. This is both woven and told. ~ YARN
D16. Product of a happy home. ~ BREW
D18. The great divide. ~ RENO
D20. Vicious yegg (init.). ~ VY
D21. Man's nickname. ~ ED
D23. Enjoy. ~ LIKE
D25. When this comes from a Christmas tree it looks like a cross between a Chinese sunset and a flaming aurora borealis. ~ TIE
D27. There's one on every fountain pen. (Whoever named them fountain pens sure said a handful.) ~ NIB
D30. Crafty. ~ SLY
D31. A short pastoral poem. ~ IDYL
D32. Three hundred and sixty-five days, nights included. ~ YEAR
D34. Anything rare or precious. ~ GEM
D37. What American tourists roll down when a ship comes to port. ~ GANGWAY
D40. King of the swat. ~ BAMBINO
D41. This is my girl's name and if fanny body wants her they can have her. ~ FANNY
D43. How the pleasingly plump used to stay. (You tell 'em, corset, you've been around the ladies!) ~ LACED
D45. Foolish. ~ SILLY
D47. The kind of a hanger you see in subways. ~ STRAP
D49. Born. ~ NEE
D50. A muffin. ~ GEM
D51. A member of a wild and woolly Indian tribe. ~ UTE
D53. An able opponent of Bryan. ~ APE
D54. A shell that wasn't what it was cracked up to be. ~ DUD
D56. Contraction of even. ~ EEN
D61. The call of a freight train. ~ TOOT
D62. To detach gradually. ~ WEAN
D65. A chorus girl's asset. ~ LEG
D67. A famous tenor. (We still hear 'em in spite of Volstead.) ~ WHISKEY
D68. Expert. ~ APT
D69. A man's name. ~ DANA
D70. Eighty-six thousand four hundred seconds. ~ DAY
D72. Receptacle for slate, dust and a little coal. ~ BIN
D73. A famous canal. (Don't ask your doctor!) ~ ERIE
D74. Funny. ~ COMIC
D76. Asiatic oxen. ~ YAKS
D78. Any meal in the army. (If you don't believe it ask any soldier.) ~ MESS
D80. The kind of a girl men don't forget. ~ MODEL
D82. A place where the inhabitants thought that stuff about an-apple-a-day was a lot of applesauce. ~ EDEN
D84. This is a Greek letter that sounds like something to eat. ~ PI
D85. Pretty rotten (init.). ~ PR
D86. To tear apart. ~ REND
D88. Pet name for an eastern brain factory. ~ ELI
D90. This is usually accompanied with a bill. ~ COO
D91. A period of time. ~ ERA
D92. The way to win a girl. ~ WOO
D94. Eleven hundred. ~ MC
D97. Ordinary yap (init.). ~ OY


